From 9cd6b1bf2dfa8c60eed459eb3af366e48d807392 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude review <review@example.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 19:24:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fit in worker processes: FitPool, used by _minimize_radDists
 and the run script

The run script fitted with a ThreadPoolExecutor. Each lmfit fit is mostly
Python, and the interpreter lock lets only one thread run Python at a time,
so threads gave little speed-up. Processes do; the script's comment noted
ProcessPoolExecutor raised errors on macOS, where workers start fresh
("spawn") and must import their function, which lived in the script.

- util_emis3D.FitPool: fits in worker processes (spawn, on every platform),
  with the worker function at module level so workers can import it. The
  pool is started on first use and reused for every evaluation time, so the
  start-up (each worker imports emis3d and Cherab, ~3.5 s here) is paid once
  per run. It starts only for at least 50 fits per worker; smaller batches
  run serially unless the pool is already up. A failing fit is returned as
  an error instead of stopping the others
- Emis3D._minimize_radDists uses it with the run config's
  numProcessorsFitting, so notebooks get the speed-up too; failures are
  summarized in one warning; Emis3D.close() stops the workers
- scripts/run_emis3D.py drops its thread pool and global data and calls
  _minimize_radDists, closing the pool at the end

Measured on one CPU (no speed-up possible there): 120 fits give identical
scores serially and through the pool; covariances survive the round trip.

Tests: tests/test_parallel.py (4), using real worker processes.
